REGIONAL REGULATION OF SEMARANG REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2015

CONCERNING

EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD.

REGENT OF SEMARANG,

Considering:

a. that the empowerment of women is carried out so that women can actualize their potential optimally to participate in development in accordance with their capacity;
b. that women as national assets who play a role in the process of continuation and creation of quality generations need to receive guarantees for the fulfillment of their rights and protection from acts of violence and discrimination in order to build society, nation and state;
c. that based on Article 12 paragraph (2) letter b of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government it is stated in essence that the empowerment of women is a mandatory affair that must be implemented by regional governments;
d. that based on Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government in the Appendix it is stated that the division of government affairs in the field of women's empowerment with one of the sub-affairs is the protection of women;
e. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a, b, c and d it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 13 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions Within the Province of Central Java;
3. Law Number 67 of 1958 concerning Amendment of the Territorial Boundaries of Salatiga Municipality and Level II Autonomous Region of Semarang (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1958 Number 118, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 1652);
4. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019);
5. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on The Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
6. Law Number 13 of 1998 concerning Elderly Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1998 Number 190, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3796);
7. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
8. Law Number 2 of 2002 concerning the State Police of the Republic of Indonesia (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 2, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4168);
9. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4279);
10. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
11. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5589), as amended by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 246);
12. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
13. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
14. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
15. Law Number 36 of 2009 concerning Health (State Gazette of 2009 Number 144, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5063);
16. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
17. Law Number 19 of 2011 concerning Ratification of the Convention On The Rights Of Persons with Disabilities (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 107, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5251);
18. Government Regulation Number 79 of 2005 concerning Guidelines for Guidance and Supervision of the Implementation of Regional Government (State Gazette of 2005 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4593);
19. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
20. Government Regulation Number 38 of 2007 concerning Division of Government Affairs between the Government, Provincial Regional Government, and Regency/City Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4737);
21. Presidential Decree Number 88 of 2002 concerning National Action Plan for the Elimination of Trafficking in Women and Children;
22. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 13 of 2006 concerning Guidelines for Regional Financial Management, as has been amended several times, most recently by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 21 of 2011 concerning Second Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 13 of 2006 concerning Guidelines for Regional Financial Management;
23. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Improving the Quality of Life of Women;
24. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Implementation of Protection of Women;
25. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment Number 4 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Community Institutions;
26. Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for Implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in the Regions as amended by Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 67 of 2011 concerning Amendment to Regulation of the Minister of Home Affairs Number 15 of 2008 concerning General Guidelines for Implementation of Gender Mainstreaming in the Regions;
27. Regional Regulation of Semarang Regency Number 16 of 2008 concerning Government Affairs That Are the Authority of the Regional Government of Semarang Regency (Regional Gazette of Semarang Regency of 2008 Number 16, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Semarang Regency Number 14);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SEMARANG REGENCY

And

REGENT OF SEMARANG

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN

CHAPTER II

SCOPE

Article 2

The scope of this Regional Regulation includes:
a. principles and objectives;
b. women's rights;
c. obligations and responsibilities of regional government;
d. women's empowerment;
e. women's protection;
f. implementation of women's protection;
g. strategy for women's empowerment and protection;
h. mechanism for organizing women's empowerment and protection;
i. financing;
j. guidance and supervision;
k. transitional provisions; and
l. closing provisions.

CHAPTER IV

WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 5

1. Every woman has the right to:
a. live and maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. fulfill her basic needs to grow and develop properly, have a family and continue offspring;
c. develop her personality, to obtain education, and improve her quality of life in order to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
d. obtain justice, security, and freedom to express opinions without discrimination;
e. be involved in every stage of the development process;
f. be free from slavery or enslavement and threats;
g. obtain protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property rights;
h. obtain welfare and a decent life;
i. participate in politics;
j. perform legal acts; and
k. freely choose a partner in her marriage.
2. Every wife during the marriage bond has the same rights and responsibilities as her husband over all matters relating to her married life, relations with her children, and ownership and management of joint property in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.
3. After the dissolution of marriage, a woman has rights and responsibilities with her former husband over all matters relating to children with due regard to the best interests of the child.

Article 8

Women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 7 letter a is implemented through:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitation and capital assistance; and
e. facilitation of marketing network development.

Article 9

The empowerment of women in the socio-cultural field as referred to in Article 7 letter b shall be implemented through:
a. enhancement of knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of tiered education in accordance with potential to improve social status;
b. enhancement of awareness, willingness and ability to overcome health problems through quality promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative efforts, primarily in the field of reproductive health;
c. enhancement of awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration; and
d. facilitation and efforts to preserve customs and the development of science, technology, arts and culture for the advancement of women.

Article 13

The implementation of protection of women shall be provided to:
a. women workers/laborers;
b. elderly women;
c. women with disabilities;
d. homeless women;
e. domestic women workers;
f. women heads of household;
g. women former residents of correctional institutions;
h. women disaster victims;
i. women commercial sex workers; and
j. women victims of violence.

Article 14

1. The implementation of protection for women workers/laborers as referred to in Article 13 letter a is to provide protection from acts of economic exploitation and cruel, inhumane treatment and to seek proper appreciation for work.
2. Women workers/laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive protection for:
a. recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. health and social security;
c. access to information and legal consultation services;
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status; and
e. witnesses and victims.

Article 17

1. The implementation of protection for homeless women as referred to in Article 13 letter d is in order to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
2. Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

CHAPTER XII

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 28

1. The Regent or SKPD in charge of women's empowerment conducts guidance and supervision over women empowerment and protection.
2. Forms of guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) are as follows:
a. providing socialization to the community and business sector regarding women empowerment and protection policies;
b. providing books, leaflets, brochures regarding women empowerment and protection, and other related issues and disseminating them to the community;
c. facilitating capacity building of institutions implementing women empowerment and protection;
d. facilitating forums for women's participation and women's organizations;
e. facilitating rehabilitation and reintegration services for women victims of disaster and victims of violence;
f. coordinating the participation of the business sector and other institutions, including international assistance for women empowerment and protection; and
g. providing awards to the community and business sector both individually and institutionally who have made efforts in women empowerment and protection.
3. Forms of supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of monitoring and evaluation activities over women empowerment and protection.
